In the ArrayList code provided, the Count property was used inside the Add method. The old count was retrieved before the addition was made and the new count was retrieved after the addition was made, therefore, the assert statement makes sure that before and after the function call (addition of an item), the count was increased by one.

Another assert statement that can be added is:
<code>
Debug.Assert(OldCapacity <= NewCapacity);
</code>
Ensuring the array capacity doesn’t decrease after resizing the ArrayList.

For AddRange method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(OldCount + c.Count == NewCount);
</code>
This assert statement checks whether after adding the elements of the collection to the ArrayList, the original count of items in the ArrayList plus the count of the provided collection equals the new count.

For the Clear method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(NewCount == 0);
Debug.Assert(Array.TrueForAll(NewArray, i => i == null));
</code>
The first assert checks that after calling Clear, the count of elements in the ArrayList is zero. The second assert checks that all elements in the inner array (underlying array) are null.

For Contains method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(NewArray.Contains(item));
</code>
This assertion checks that the item to be contained is in the ArrayList.

For IndexOf method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.IndexOf(OldArray, item, OldCount) == index);
</code>
This assertion checks that, in the old state of ArrayList, the index returned from IndexOf is as expected.

For Insert method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(OldCount + 1 == NewCount);
Debug.Assert(NewArray[index] == item);
</code>
The first assertion ensures that the count of the elements in the ArrayList increases when an item is inserted. The second assertion checks that the inserted item actually ends up in the correct location.

For the LastIndexOf method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(index >= 0 && index < OldCount);
Debug.Assert(OldArray[index] == item);
Debug.Assert(OldArray[index] == NewArray[NewCount - 1]);
</code>
These asserts determine that the item is indeed in the last index of the ArrayList, its existence in the array, and its value after LastIndexOf method operation.

For Remove method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(OldCount - 1 == NewCount);
</code>
This simple assertion checks that the element to be removed indeed is removed from the ArrayList.

For RemoveAt method:
<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.FindIndex(NewArray, 0, count, o => Equals(o, OldArray[index])) == -1);
</code>
This assert checks that there is indeed no element at the removed index.

For the methods to resize the ArrayList, ensure that the capacity is increased accordingly when items are added to the list. If not, they should be added.

Note: I used the Debug.Assert method here for demonstration purposes and it won't cause any runtime errors. It just reminds developers running a debug version of the code about potential bugs. In a production environment, you'd replace Debug.Assert with custom error handling.
To properly perform the unit testing, you should create mock versions of the arraylist and its dependencies(if any) to isolate and test the actual methods in isolation.--------------------
